# Page object rules

Apply to `framework/pages/**/*.ts`.

## Responsibility

- **Locators** — via `Selectors` from `@constants/selectors`
- **UI actions** — atomic interactions only
- **No assertions** — except established completion helpers called from specs (e.g. `CheckoutCompletePage.expectThankYouMessage`)
- **No business logic** — multi-step journeys belong in `framework/flows/`
- **No flows** — pages do not import flows

## Structure

```typescript
import { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';
import { Selectors } from '@constants/selectors';

export class ExamplePage {
  readonly usernameInput: Locator;

  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {
    this.usernameInput = page.getByTestId(Selectors.login.username);
  }
}
```

- Constructor: `constructor(private readonly page: Page)`
- Prefer **`readonly` locators** in constructor when reused across methods
- Use `page.getByTestId(Selectors.<namespace>.<key>)` or legacy `loginSelectors` exports

## Methods

- Methods must be **atomic** (one user action per method)
- Good: `fillUsername()`, `fillPassword()`, `clickLogin()`, `addBackpackToCart()`
- Bad: `completeCheckout()`, `loginAndCheckout()` — use **Flows** instead
- Return `Promise<void>` unless returning data
- Navigation: `page.goto('/')` — `baseURL` is configured

## Locators

- Do not hardcode `data-test` strings — use `Selectors`
- Forbidden: XPath, `nth-child`, brittle CSS

## Existing pages (reuse, do not duplicate)

`LoginPage`, `InventoryPage`, `CartPage`, `CheckoutPage`, `CheckoutCompletePage`

When extending `LoginPage`, prefer new atomic methods alongside existing `login()` rather than a second login implementation elsewhere.
